      "text": "To find \\(\\sin F\\) in the right triangle \\(DEF\\), where \\(\\sin D = \\frac{5}{13}\\) and \\(\\sin E = 1\\), we can use the fact that in a right triangle, the sum of the angles is \\(180^\\circ\\) and the sine of a right angle is 1.\n\nFirst, identify the angles in the right triangle. Since the sum of the angles in a triangle is \\(180^\\circ\\) and one of the angles is a right angle (\\(90^\\circ\\)), the sum of the other two angles must be \\(90^\\circ\\). Let's denote the angles as \\(D\\), \\(E\\), and \\(F\\), where \\(D\\) and \\(E\\) are complementary (i.e., \\(D + E = 90^\\circ\\)) because their sines are complementary in a right triangle.\n\nGiven:\n- \\(\\sin D = \\frac{5}{13}\\)\n- \\(\\sin E = 1\\)\n\nSince \\(\\sin E = 1\\), angle \\(E\\) must be \\(90^\\circ\\) because the sine of the right angle is always 1. Therefore, angle \\(D\\) must be \\(0^\\circ\\), but this is not possible in a triangle. This means there was an error in the interpretation of the problem or the angles given are not typical in most right triangles.\n\nHowever, if we assume the problem meant to have a right triangle with a different configuration or a different interpretation of the sines, let's consider the possibility that \\(\\sin D = 1\\) and \\(\\sin E = \\frac{5}{13}\\). This would imply that angle \\(D\\) is \\(90^\\circ\\) and angle \\(E\\) is \\(45^\\circ\\).\n\nGiven that \\(\\sin E = 1\\), angle \\(E\\) must be \\(90^\\circ\\), but this is not possible in a triangle unless the triangle is degenerate or the given sines do not correspond to a valid triangle with angles \\(0^\\circ\\) and \\(90^\\circ\\).\n\nLet's re-evaluate the problem by assuming the correct interpretation of the angles in a typical right triangle where \\(\\sin D = \\frac{5}{13}\\) and \\(\\sin E = 1\\). This would imply that angle \\(D\\) is \\(36.87^\\circ\\) and angle \\(E\\) is \\(42.79^\\circ\\) (since \\(\\sin E = \\cos D\\) and \\(\\sin D = \\frac{5}{13}\\)).\n\nThen, angle \\(F\\) would be the remaining angle in triangle \\(DEF\\), which would be:\n\\[ F = 180^\\circ - 90^\\circ - (36.87^\\circ + 42.79^\\circ) = 180^\\circ - 90^\\circ - 79.66^\\circ = 10^\\circ \\]\n\nThus, \\(\\sin F = \\sin 10^\\circ = \\frac{\\sqrt{30-13\\sqrt{30}}}{13}\\).\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\(\\boxed{\\frac{\\sqrt{30-13\\sqrt{30}}}{13}}\\)",
