      "text": "Since $DEF$ is a right triangle, angle $D$ is $90^\\circ$.  Angle $E$ is then an acute angle such that\n\n$$\n\\sin E = \\sin (180^\\circ - D - E) = \\sin (D + E) = \\sin D \\cos E + \\cos D \\sin E.\n$$\n\nSince $DE$ is the hypotenuse ($\\sin E = 1$, moreover) and $DE = 13$:\n\n$$\n\\sin D = \\frac{5}{13}, \\quad \\cos D = \\sqrt{1 - (\\frac{5}{13})^2} = \\frac{12}{13}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\sin F = \\sin (90^\\circ - D - E)$\n\n$$\n\\sin F = \\cos D \\sin E = \\frac{12}{13} \\cdot 1 = \\boxed{\\frac{12}{13}} \\, .\n$$\n\nAlternatively, since the sine of an angle in a triangle is simply the length of the side opposite that angle divided by the length of the hypotenuse, $\\sin(180^\\circ - \\frac{\\pi}{9})$ (the angle not labeled) is exactly the length of the side opposite it divided by the hypotenuse.  Therefore, the angle opposite the side labeled \"13\" is $\\frac{\\pi}{2}$ (in radians) or $90^\\circ$.  This means the third angle is $90^\\circ - 90^\\circ$, or $0^\\circ$ radians, meaning that:\n\n$$\n\\sin F = \\sin 0^\\circ \\, .\n$$\n\nTherefore $\\boxed{0}$",
